    "created_at": "2025-12-27T14:06:48Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-12-27T14:06:55Z",
    "body": "Hi, not sure if feedback is still considered at this stage. And this is quite a niche case but I would still like to mention it here. On Windows it seems there is a common pattern used by software to update a file, e.g. when the user clicks \"save\": First create a temporary file with the new contents, then delete the actual file, then rename the temporary file to the actual file. The deletion step is necessary because the rename can fail if there is a process that has an open file handle to the destination file. E.g. Word does this: https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/topic/description-of-how-word-creates-temporary-files-66b112fb-d2c0-8f40-a0be-70a367cc4c85\r\n\r\nFor this API this means that if you watch a file for changes the observer registration is destroyed at the deletion step and you won't be able to receive any more updates. One option to work around this would be to add a \"debounce\" to the spec that does not destroy the observer registration if the path appears again within a short time frame.",
